WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:06.830
BRADLEY WONG: Hi.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.870
Welcome to Getting
Started with Anthos.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:10.970
My name is Bradley Wong,
and I'm a product manager

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.720
at Google Cloud.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.720
AURELIE FONTENY: And my
name is Aurelie Fonteny,

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.910
and I'm a product
manager for Anthos.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.640
BRADLEY WONG: So
today the agenda

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.720
we're going to go
through is we're

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.475
going to start with an
introduction of what Anthos is,

00:00:26.475 --> 00:00:28.990
we're going to go through
some of the use cases

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:32.360
that our customers have seen
in the last 12 months of using

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.840
Anthos, and then
we're going to go

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:41.210
through some suggestions on
how to get started with Anthos.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.300
So what is Anthos?

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.630
Anthos has been around
for more than 12 months

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:50.500
now in the open, so
a lot of customers

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.990
have asked us, well, you
know, what is Anthos?

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.850
Is it GKE On-Prem?

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:55.960
Is it GKE?

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.240
Is it a container as
a service platform?

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.370
Is it a platform as a service?

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.860
Is it just a bunch
of open source tools?

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.340
Is it a bundle of different
tools that Google's offering,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.290
or is it all of the above?

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.530
Well, probably a good way
to start with a description

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.140
is to actually start
with the statement that

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.210
describes what Anthos is.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.523
Now, this is not all
the different use cases

00:01:20.523 --> 00:01:22.190
the Anthos can provide,
but it certainly

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:25.610
is a core set of how
we think about Anthos.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:30.770
So Anthos is a managed
platform for enterprises

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:34.010
that want faster modernization
and greater consistency

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:37.640
in a multi and
hybrid cloud world.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.160
That's a lot of words, so let's
break that down a little bit.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.510
First, managed platform.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.730
What do we mean by that?

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:51.180
Well, first of all, the platform
could be consumed or subscribed

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.510
to.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:56.770
Our customers can consume it
as they want, as they need,

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:00.670
as the requirements
grow, or they can also

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.650
purchase an upfront subscription
to offset any of the usage

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.580
charges as well.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.020
Next, a managed platform
is also something

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.900
that takes away the toil of
actually managing it yourself.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.850
So in that sense, Google
actually manages that for you.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.750
So what we provide is
the capability for you

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:26.740
to incrementally upgrade the
capabilities of the platform

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.170
as you deem necessary.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:33.010
And any updates to versions
to fix vulnerabilities,

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:36.400
to fix bugs, or add
new features is also

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.110
managed by Google as well.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:44.650
So next, what do we mean
by application platform?

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.450
In this sense and
application platform

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:49.180
provides something
that can actually

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:54.180
host the applications
of your choice, be they

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.970
container based applications,
or they can also

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.190
be traditional applications.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:03.960
Our application platform is
based on completely open source

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.910
projects.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:10.800
For example, Kubernetes, Istio,
Knative, Tekton, to name a few.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.890
Now the platform
is also designed

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:21.510
for enterprises in mind that
have large complex needs.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.330
They could be
security requirements,

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:27.520
operational requirements,
governance requirements.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.560
There's many, many requirements
that our enterprises need

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.330
to cater for, and Anthos
provides all those enterprises

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:40.220
with the capability to manage
all those needs, be they

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.850
large or small.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:46.990
Next, our customers also want
the ability to have access

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.420
to faster modernization.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.940
And with Anthos,
we provide tooling

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:54.340
to help our customers
to have no touch

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.310
migrations from
traditional workloads

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.500
into modern workloads.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.090
And not just the
actual applications

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:04.450
themselves, but
also the platform,

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.590
and we provide tools to
help our customers modernize

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.690
the way they even manage
the platform itself.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:18.440
Next, Anthos provides
greater consistency.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:22.100
It's very common that across
our enterprise customers

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.990
that they may deploy Anthos in
not just a single deployment

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.900
option like in GCP,
but may want to deploy

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.540
this across hybrid
environments as well

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.040
as multi-cloud environments.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.210
So in that sense, the importance
to have a single pane of glass

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:40.460
and a single
definition of how they

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:46.010
want security policies or even
just general configurations

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:49.370
need to be kept in sync
across all those environments,

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:54.330
and Anthos provides
methods to achieve that.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:05:00.150
Finally, Anthos is a multi-cloud
and hybrid-cloud platform.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.830
It's completely
software based, which

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.640
means it runs inside
on-premises data centers,

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:11.070
inside GCP, and also
in other clouds.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:14.650
Now, Anthos those
didn't happen overnight.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:16.650
It is actually a culmination
of decade's worth

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:19.200
of work from Google.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.410
There's no secret that Google
has been using containers

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.360
for many, many years.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.620
And even today, Google
still leverages the father

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.530
of Kubernetes, which
is something internally

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:38.460
we call [? Borg. ?] Now,
in the early or mid 2010s,

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.660
so in the 2013, 2014
time frame, Google open

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:45.990
sourced a project called
Kubernetes to the world, which

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.150
had a lot of the
building blocks stemming

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.750
from our experiences
of operating container

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.780
environments at scale.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.390
Kubernetes is now one of the
most successful open source

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:01.010
projects in the industry.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:05.600
Now, we saw that Kubernetes
although was very powerful,

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.930
it was actually
quite complex to use.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:12.620
So we released in
2015 something we

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:15.560
call GKE, which
allowed our customers

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:19.280
to not have to think
about how to install

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.410
and manage Kubernetes.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:26.660
We, as Google, manage it for
you, and GKE is that vehicle.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:29.840
Today, on a daily
basis, we see billions

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:35.470
of containers deployed
onto GKE by customers

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.130
all around the world.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:42.560
So then naturally in 2018,
because of the demand

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.270
to use GKE, we saw that there
was also an increased demand

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:50.780
to use GKE not just inside
Google Cloud's environment,

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:55.840
but also in all other
environments as well.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:01.660
We started with GKE On-Prem
in 2018, and now with Anthos,

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:06.100
we expanded to multiple
different environments.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:12.010
So in 2019 at NEXT, Anthos was
announced by CEO Sundar Pichai

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:14.950
to be generally available.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:21.120
We are now 12 months into the
general availability of Anthos,

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.680
and this is where
Anthos sits today.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:27.800
Anthos is now able
to be deployed

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:30.350
across multiple
different environments,

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.405
whether it's on
premises data centers,

00:07:32.405 --> 00:07:36.950
in computing environments,
Google Cloud, other clouds,

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:40.080
and also other
Kubernetes clusters.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.390
It also provides multiple
different services on top,

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:47.400
whether it's cluster
management, service management,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.960
providing developers with more
agility through serverless

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:54.450
and app development frameworks,
manage operations, as well as

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.420
policy and configuration
management.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:00.300
What this translates
to concretely

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:03.000
for the different components
and deployment options of Anthos

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.670
is this.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:10.360
We now have multiple different
deployment options, so On-Prem,

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:14.420
bare metal, which is coming
into preview this quarter.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:20.480
On GCP, we recently made
available Anthos in AWS.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.930
By the end of this year we
also have Azure VMs becoming

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:25.970
available, and we
also just announced

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:27.560
the general
availability of Anthos

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:31.160
attach clusters to allow our
customers to deploy Anthos

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:33.872
on other Kubernetes
environments,

00:08:33.872 --> 00:08:35.539
essentially bringing
your own Kubernetes

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:37.860
environment to Anthos.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.070
Now, Anthos then provides
a single pane of glass

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:43.010
to manage all of
those environments,

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:47.810
but on top of that, provides
opinionated components

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:50.690
to enable additional services.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:54.590
GKE for container management,
and the service mesh

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:58.880
to manage a service mesh
deployments powered by SDO.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:03.630
Cloud Run to provide a
serverless framework,

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:08.790
Cloud Code to
provide IDE enabled

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:13.260
development environments, cloud
logging and cloud monitoring

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.550
to provide operational
visibility across all

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:20.160
those deployment
options, whether it's

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.250
for the platform itself
or for the applications,

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.240
as well as Anthos
config management,

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:28.110
which provides
consistency of deploying

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:33.160
policies and configurations
across all those environments.

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:36.510
So now let's take
a moment on looking

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:40.195
at what some of our customers
are using Anthos for.

00:09:40.195 --> 00:09:42.070
So let's have a look at
some of the use cases

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:47.240
that Anthos has enabled
over the past 12 months.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:49.180
The first one that
we find most commonly

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:54.430
is customers who want to migrate
and modernize their application

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:56.340
stacks.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.560
Many of these
customers really want

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.560
to enable their
developers to move faster,

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.780
and their reliance on legacy
applications and their stacks

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:10.860
have been an impedance to be
able to offer better business

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.160
agility.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:16.230
So when the promise of
containers has come up,

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:20.640
that was obviously a very
appealing path to choose,

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:23.340
but it was very difficult
for our customers to do that

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:25.460
without a lot of refactoring.

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:28.320
Anthos provides our
customers the tooling

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:31.200
and the capabilities to
help them migrate now

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.950
from legacy applications into
more modern container based

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:39.867
environments with
limited refactoring.

00:10:39.867 --> 00:10:41.450
We also have customers
who are already

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:45.680
experts at using containers,
Kubernetes, and some of them

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:48.800
are already GKE customers,
but many of them

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:51.230
have also on
premises requirements

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:55.250
because they can't move their
workloads into cloud today,

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:58.670
so many of them are looking
at Anthos to actually

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:03.110
be that next generation
environmental container

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:05.450
platform.

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:08.450
Variety reasons why they
they're looking at that,

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:12.110
but the ease of use, the
consistency, the cost,

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:14.570
the flexibility,
all of those come

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.813
to head when they're evaluating
some of the new platforms

00:11:17.813 --> 00:11:18.980
that they want to invest in.

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:21.620
And Anthos has
time and time again

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:24.470
been a platform of choice
for a lot of our customers

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:27.890
who are coming from
some existing Kubernetes

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:29.970
environments.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:31.760
We also have a lot
of customers who

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.370
are coming from
interesting places

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:38.570
such as in the retail space or
in the Telco space who really

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:42.320
want to use Anthos as a
means to deploy clusters

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.750
and platforms in places where
you might not expect them

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:47.900
to exist, whether they
are inside retail stores

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:53.990
or locations inside ballparks,
or next to Telco 5G towers.

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:55.580
A lot of our
customers really want

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:59.090
to move analytic applications
closer and closer

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:02.870
to those environments too get
closer to their customers.

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:04.750
And finally, we have
a lot of customers

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:11.290
who really value the benefits
of some of the intelligent GCP

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:13.020
services that are offered.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.900
Now, some of them may
not have the ability

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:20.080
to move those workloads
into a cloud environment

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:23.560
yet for regulatory or
compliance reasons,

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.590
so rather than asking
our customers to move

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:30.280
their data into GCP
today, what we are doing

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:33.400
is moving the
intelligent services

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:38.580
like AI and ML services into
their on premises environments,

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:40.710
and Anthos provides them
the ability to do that.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:48.950
So also consistently across all
of those different use cases,

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:51.350
we have customers
who not just want

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:53.660
to modernize the
application stacks,

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:57.620
but also want to modernize
how they are doing operations.

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:01.880
So GitOps is a common
theme across most

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:06.240
of the customers using git
based mechanism software

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.780
continuous delivery
type of methods

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:12.230
to not just provide
continuous deployments

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:16.370
of their applications into
dev staging production,

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:19.610
but also to do that for their
infrastructure definitions

00:13:19.610 --> 00:13:20.540
as well.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:25.940
So leveraging git controls to
describe their configurations

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:32.870
for the platform allows them to
mutate, modify, check in check

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:36.560
out configurations
and be able to deploy

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:38.990
different versions of the
application stack at scale.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:43.960
In light of that,
many other customers

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:46.360
then will take that
advantage and be

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:52.570
able to stamp out verified
and certified versions

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:55.840
of the configurations that
they deem as important

00:13:55.840 --> 00:14:00.140
for different security zones
inside those environments.

00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:04.060
Some of them may have small
stringent security settings

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:06.400
in, say, for example,
production of staging,

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.010
but less stringent
security environments

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:11.320
for development and testing.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:14.770
With the GitOps model
and with configures data,

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:17.320
it's possible to
ensure versioning,

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:20.410
and it's possible to ensure the
correct security postures have

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:23.500
been rolled out with the right
configurations across all

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:26.600
those different environments.

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:30.140
Finally, many of our
customers are also

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:34.700
ISVs who want to have
the most frictionless way

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:38.150
to rollout their software
platform without having

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:42.350
to build an entirely new
infrastructure or application

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:44.010
stack themselves.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:48.200
So Anthos offers a lot
of our ISV partners

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:51.520
the ability to take
their software platform

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:55.910
and to deliver a software as
a service, a SAS solution,

00:14:55.910 --> 00:15:01.280
for a lot of those
solutions as well.

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:04.540
So with that, I'm now going
to hand over to my colleague

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:06.850
Aurelia who's going to
walk you through how

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:09.470
to get started with Anthos.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:11.060
AURELIE FONTENY:
Thank you, Brad.

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:13.820
So I will now walk you
through the specifics

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.570
on how to get started.

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:19.310
So the first thing is you want
to learn more about Anthos,

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:23.300
so you can go and start with
the Anthos documentation, Anthos

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:28.460
specific Qwiklabs,
and Coursera training.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:31.070
Then, the next
step after that is

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:34.640
to try Anthos in
your own environment.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:38.480
So for Anthos running
on GCP, we currently

